Before reading #422 I was not aware of the existence of `Whitebox`. I am more inclined to completely remove this class and not support/allow such (in my opinion) hacks instead of expanding the features in this class. This is my 2 cents, but I am curious what the others are thinking.
You cannot get 100% code coverage without it. The [Stack Overflow](http://stackoverflow.com/questions/2474017/using-reflection-to-change-static-final-file-separatorchar-for-unit-testing/2474242#2474242) question and answer on setting private static final fields is quite popular. I used this class on several projects and I find it quite useful.
Sometimes I simply need to mock/test other libraries that I don't have any control of. Whitebox is really useful in these situations.
I really hope you do not remove existing functionality.
I am not a big fan of this class either. And to be honest, 100% test coverage is not really something that should be attempted in my opinion. Note that the class is in an internal package and is not intended to be exposed to outside users.
100% test coverage is not a goal in itself. I fully agree with @raphw . @andreicristianpetcu are you testing getters and setters too? If anybody asks me for an opinion - I'm strongly against merging this PR.
We have 100% code coverage. We either use the getters and setter or we don't. If we do, we do both in production and in the tests the same way and if we don't we delete them. 100% code coverage is not that hard to do. We are required to code like this.

My code does not look like the one in [your comment](https://github.com/mockito/mockito/issues/422#issuecomment-222663244) @marcingrzejszczak.

Mine is more like 

``` java
class YourClass {
    private final LoggerProxy proxy;

    YourClass(LoggerProxy proxy) {
        this.proxy = proxy;
    }

    void someMethod() {
        loggerProxy.debug(...); // <-- this you can stub
    }
}
```

``` java
class LoggerProxy {

    private static final Logger log ....

    void debug(...) { 
    if(log.isDebugEnabled()){
        Object computedObject = computeComplicatedDebugInfo();
        log.debug(..., computedObject);
    }
}
```

I need to cover the `if(log.isDebugEnabled()){` branch since I am building complex business around logging. Logging for me is the core domain. In any case I still need to mock the proxy `private static final Logger log`. I don't just use `log.debug("my object {}", computedObject);` since when logging complex objects it is better to check if it is debug or not before building the object.

Take the example in the [StackOverflow thread](http://stackoverflow.com/questions/2474017/using-reflection-to-change-static-final-file-separatorchar-for-unit-testing/2474242#2474242). That user want to mock the file system of the operating system so tests can be run on one platform but they will test logic of multiple platforms.

I tried to fix my issue with Powermock but it does not work either since it does bytecode manipulation and I can only mock `LogFactory.getLog(ClassToTest.class)` which is called exactly once for each class and I need to reuse the mock if I have more tests and this really feels dirty.

@TimvdLippe You might not be aware of the existence of the Whitebox class but Mockito's users are. 

I'm sorry for wasting your time with this. I was thinking this was a simple merge request with no huge debate. If you don't want to accept it, I will not insist. I was hoping I would improve the project and also fix my issue. Thank you for your time.
Andrei, I can very much understand your use case and it is a legitimate approach to use reflection for it. The problem is simply that Mockito is concerned with logging, not with reflection which you can rather represent in a dedicated utility within your test code. 

The `Whitebox` class is meant for internal use and not as an exposed API that Mockito supports. The problem with adding is that by adding this to the public API would require us to support it. In the future, the scope of the reflection API might also change with Jigsaw which would make this even harder in the future.

Thank you for understanding, we really appreciate your effort!
@andreicristianpetcu your pull request is very much appreciated! I have no objections to the implementation, but more the reason behind it. This debate is more a debate on the front of: How much should Mockito be able to do? Previously we had discussions on dependency injection where Mockito seemed to turn into a dependency framework, here we allow full reflection on a class and the (imo) bad practices associated with them.

I wonder if we can gather some data on the usage of the Whitebox class and see if there is a whitespread usage or only a few developers are relying on it.
@TimvdLippe you can find it on Github [4,602 code results](https://github.com/search?l=java&q=org.mockito.internal.util.reflection.Whitebox&type=Code&utf8=%E2%9C%93) for Java language and the package + class.
